 In C programming, there are several ways to handle errors and return them to the caller. Here's one common approach using error codes:

1. Define error codes:

```c
#define SUCCESS 0
#define ERROR_INVALID_INPUT -1
#define ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY -2
#define ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND -3
```

2. Use error codes in functions:

```c
int function_name(...) {
    // Function implementation
    if (some_condition_is_not_met) {
        return ERROR_INVALID_INPUT;
    }

    // If memory allocation fails
    if (!(result = malloc(size))) {
        return ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;
    }

    // If file not found
    if (fopen(filename, "r") == NULL) {
        free(result);
        return ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND;
    }

    // Function continues with success
    // ...

    // Function returns success
    free(result);
    return SUCCESS;
}
```

3. Check the return value in the calling function:

```c
int main() {
    int result = function_name(...);

    if (result != SUCCESS) {
        switch (result) {
            case ERROR_INVALID_INPUT:
                printf("Invalid input\n");
                break;
            case ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY:
                printf("Out of memory\n");
                break;
            case ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND:
                printf("File not found\n");
                break;
            default:
                printf("Unknown error: %d\n", result);
        }
        return 1;
    }

    // Function call was successful
    // ...

    return 0;
}
```

This approach allows you to return an error code from a function and check it in the calling function to handle the error appropriately. You can define more error codes as needed for your specific use case.

