 To create a new line within a cell in a LaTeX table, you can use the `\\` command. However, LaTeX interprets `\\` as a command to start a new row, so you'll need to use the `parbox` command to create a multi-line cell. Here's an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tabularx}

\begin{document}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{|X|}
\hline
\textbf{Cell with Multiple Lines} \\
\hline
\parbox{.8\linewidth}{
Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.
Integer nec odio. Praesent libero.
Sed cursus ante dapibus diam.
Sed nisi. Nulla quis sem at nibh elementum imperdiet.
Duis sagittis ipsum. Praesent mauris.}
\\
\hline
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

In this example, the `\parbox{.8\linewidth}{...}` command creates a box that takes up 80% of the line width, and the content within it is treated as multiple lines. The `\\` command at the end of the content signifies a new line within the cell.

